From a7134b2b1d2256e3660810e6bf9628e7b23702b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 16:30:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/94] cpufreq: qcom-hw: msm8998: program the APM threshold
 corner in SEQ_REG1

On MSM8998v2 the OSM sequencer takes the Array Power Mux threshold
virtual corner - the first corner whose open-loop voltage requires the
APM to hand the memory arrays over from MX to APC - from a register in
the non-secure page at OSM base + 0x1048, which is the same location as
this driver's setup_regs.reg_seq1 (freq-domain base 0x179c1000 + 0x48).
On v1 silicon that quantity goes to the TrustZone owned SEQ_REG(1)
instead, and 0x1048 is not part of the APM sequence at all.

The downstream driver for this SoC writes apm_threshold_vc there:

  drivers/clk/msm/clock-osm.c:81     #define SEQ_REG1_MSM8998_V2 0x1048
  drivers/clk/msm/clock-osm.c:2420   } else if (msm8998_v2) {
                                             clk_osm_write_reg(c,
                                                 c->apm_threshold_vc,
                                                 SEQ_REG1_MSM8998_V2);

(msm-4.4 wahoo, drivers/clk/msm/clock-osm.c; the same function writes
apm_threshold_vc to SEQ_REG(15) and apm_crossover_vc to SEQ_REG(72),
which is what apm_vc and num_entries map to here.)

We write num_entries - the APM crossover corner, which CPRh appends at
the end of the table - so the sequencer is told the MX to APC switch
happens one corner past the last DVFS entry, while SEQ_REG(15) gets the
correct threshold from the same function. Every table corner then sits
below the threshold as far as the sequencer is concerned, and the two
slots disagree.

Write apm_vc, matching both the vendor driver and the value this driver
already puts in SEQ_APM_THRESH_VC. The crossover corner keeps going to
SEQ_REG(72) unchanged.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
index 20112af41..f05f8c62c 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
@@ -106,7 +106,7 @@
  * @reg_llm_freq_vote_hyst: DCVS-LLM Wait time for frequency inc/decrement
  * @reg_llm_volt_vote_hyst: DCVS-LLM Wait time for voltage inc/decrement
  * @reg_llm_intf_dcvs_dis:  DCVS-LLM en/disable control
- * @reg_seq1:               Sequencer extra register
+ * @reg_seq1:               Sequencer APM threshold Virtual Corner
  * @reg_pdn_fsm_ctrl:       Boost and Droop FSMs en/disable control
  * @reg_cc_boost_timer:     CC-Boost FSM wait first timer register
  * @reg_dcvs_boost_timer:   DCVS-Boost FSM wait first timer register
@@ -1023,12 +1023,19 @@ static int qcom_cpufreq_hw_write_lut(struct device *cpu_dev,
 	if (apm_vc == INT_MAX)
 		apm_vc = LUT_MAX_ENTRIES - 1;
 
+	/*
+	 * On MSM8998v2 the APM threshold corner is taken from a sequencer
+	 * register that lives in the non-secure page instead of from the
+	 * TrustZone owned SEQ_REG(1), so it holds the same value as the
+	 * SEQ_APM_THRESH_VC slot programmed below.
+	 */
+	writel(apm_vc, ddata->base + sregs->reg_seq1);
+
 	/*
 	 * APM crossover virtual corner refers to CPRh: there, the APM corner
 	 * is always appended to the table (so, at the end of it, right after
 	 * the cluster dvfs entries).
 	 */
-	writel(num_entries, ddata->base + sregs->reg_seq1);
 	ret = qcom_scm_io_writel(SEQUENCER_REG(seq_addr, SEQ_APM_CROSSOVER_VC), num_entries);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
